+# Desktop meeting capture — implementation plan
+
+**Forge issue:** `1ff834c9-d40a-4ff2-9095-b3ef9d11ff59`
+
+**Plan owner:** `codex-desktop-capture-plan`
+
+**Plan base:** `origin/main@341caeb0072f6642ce9b2172c1d092f91bcd3265`
+
+**Classification:** Critical — native audio, credentials, local encrypted data, signed distribution
+
+**Stage:** PLAN only; implementation, push, PR creation, release, and issue release are not authorized
+
+## Outcome
+
+Ship a small, user-started Tauri 2 recorder for Windows and macOS that captures microphone and system audio, durably forwards short chunks to the existing Product-Suite meeting ingestion/evidence/finalization path, and survives network loss or process crashes without silently losing or duplicating audio.
+
+The collector is deliberately not a bot or a second Product-Suite application. It has no local AI, summary, candidate, task, run, tenant, or database authority. Calendar data may suggest an association, but only a visible user action starts recording.
+
+## Superseded direction and scope
+
+The older issue text requiring Electron is stale. The newer product roadmap and recorded issue decisions select a thin Tauri collector and reject Electron because its bundled Chromium process is inconsistent with the memory target. DEV must implement Tauri 2; changing back to Electron is a product decision that returns to PLAN.
+
+MVP supports:
+
+- Windows 10 1703+ and Windows 11 on x86_64, using native Rust WASAPI shared-mode loopback for system audio.
+- macOS 14.2+ on Apple Silicon and Intel, using Core Audio Process Tap for system audio, subject to the physical-device proof gate below.
+- Microphone capture on both platforms, with source meters and an explicit preflight.
+- Manual start, pause/resume, stop, discard, recovery, and optional user-confirmed calendar association.
+
+Linux is explicitly deferred. It returns only after a filed demand/evidence decision names supported distributions, proves PipeWire/PulseAudio capture and WebKitGTK packaging on physical systems, assigns signing/update ownership, and accepts a CI/release matrix. No Linux feature flags, placeholder backend, package target, or capability is part of these PRs.
+
+## Existing boundaries to reuse
+
+This plan does not replace or pre-empt the following lanes:
+
+- Local durability issue `d88d461a-c259-4dc6-a751-f2b3c6898b7a` owns the server contract for persist-before-send slices, idempotent `(capture_session_id, sequence)` acknowledgement, retry/drain, and contiguous-sequence finalization safety.
+- Terminal finalization issue `ce942338-35af-4960-ae83-ebc55bdbbe2a` owns canonical terminal states, final partial materialization, rendered-artifact refresh, and `POST /meetings/{id}/finalize`.
+- The external meeting connector plan owns the `LocalCaptureSource` capability/evidence adapter and `MeetingEvidenceEnvelope`, including gaps, media topology, consent, provenance, deletion, availability, and speaker uncertainty.
+- Caller-authority meeting work exclusively owns candidate derivation, versions, `Add`/`Adjust`/`Dismiss`, task promotion, and review UI.
+- Product-Suite auth and meeting APIs remain the only tenant/user and meeting-data authority. The desktop app may cache an opaque session credential in the OS credential store; it never derives tenant identity from local input.
+
+Before each dependent DEV PR begins, rebase on current `origin/main`, record its exact base SHA, and verify the prerequisite contract is landed. If it is not landed, stop that PR rather than inventing a parallel endpoint or compatibility shim.
+
+## Architecture
+
+```text
+user click + permission preflight
+             |
+   +---------+----------+
+   |                    |
+WASAPI loopback /   Core Audio Tap /       microphone
+Windows Rust        macOS Rust             platform input
+   |                    |                       |
+   +---------------- timestamped source frames-+
+                            |
+                 5–10 second chunk encoder
+                 (source topology preserved)
+                            |
+             encrypt -> fsync -> atomic rename
+                            |
+               application-private spool
+                            |
+             authenticated Product-Suite API
+          `(capture_session_id, sequence, digest)`
+                            |
+             durable server ack / idempotent replay
+                            |
+              delete local ciphertext after ack
+                            |
+     stop -> drain contiguous ack -> canonical finalize
+                            |
+              existing evidence/artifact pipeline
+```
+
+The Tauri webview renders a small control/status surface. All privileged work—audio, keys, spool, authentication token access, upload, updater invocation—stays in Rust commands with narrow typed inputs. The webview cannot choose filesystem paths, execute a shell, fetch arbitrary URLs, read secrets, or call unrestricted HTTP.
+
+### Native audio choice
+
+Windows system audio uses WASAPI loopback through the `windows` crate and COM, not a browser API or a generic abstraction that cannot prove loopback semantics. Capture the default render endpoint in shared mode; observe endpoint changes and recover without reusing stale handles. Microphone and system frames carry monotonic timestamps and distinct `source_kind` values. Do not claim speaker identity from mixed system audio.
+
+macOS system audio uses Core Audio Process Tap on macOS 14.2+. Apple documents audio taps as an audio-only way to capture outgoing audio from processes or process groups, with an OS permission prompt and `NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription`. It is a closer least-privilege match than ScreenCaptureKit, whose screen-capture permission and visual stream surface are unnecessary for this recorder. PR 1 has a blocking physical-device spike on Intel and Apple Silicon for permission first-run/denial/revocation, aggregate device creation, device changes, sleep/wake, silence, one-hour stability, and simultaneous mic capture. If that spike cannot meet the gate, stop and return to PLAN for a bounded ScreenCaptureKit alternative; do not silently add it as a fallback. Pre-14.2 macOS is outside MVP for the same reason.
+
+Capture keeps mic and system audio as separately identified tracks/channels through the upload contract when the landed durability API supports it. If the landed API only accepts one audio body, the upstream contract must first add an explicit topology-preserving representation. The desktop app must not locally collapse sources, infer speakers, or add an undocumented multiplexing format.
+
+### Collector state machine
+
+Allowed states are `signed_out`, `permission_required`, `ready`, `recording`, `paused`, `stopping`, `draining`, `complete`, `recoverable`, `discarding`, and `error`.
+
+- Only a foreground `Start` action may transition `ready -> recording`.
+- A calendar suggestion may populate a proposed association but cannot invoke capture or a privileged Tauri command.
+- Every chunk transitions `captured -> encrypted_and_fsynced -> upload_eligible -> in_flight -> acknowledged -> deleted`.
+- Failed, timed-out, 429, or 5xx uploads return to `upload_eligible` with bounded exponential backoff and full jitter; honor `Retry-After` and cap concurrency.
+- 401/403, tenant mismatch, invalid acknowledgement, sequence/digest conflict, invalid TLS, missing key, spool corruption, or updater-signature failure is fail-closed. Preserve recoverable ciphertext and show a blocking status; never mark acknowledged or finalize.
+- `Stop` closes capture, writes the final partial chunk, and enters `draining`. Completion occurs only after the server reports the expected contiguous sequence through the terminal-finalization contract.
+- Startup enumerates encrypted manifests. Valid sessions become `recoverable` and may resume upload after authentication; corrupt or keyless sessions are quarantined and require explicit discard. Recovery never resumes audio capture automatically.
+- `Discard` requires confirmation, sends the existing deletion/cancellation signal when online, removes the OS-stored session key, securely removes best-effort local ciphertext, and records only non-sensitive local diagnostics.
+
+### Encrypted spool and credential handling
+
+Use an application-private directory and an encrypted, versioned per-session manifest—not a second product database. Each session receives a random 256-bit content key stored only through macOS Keychain or Windows DPAPI/Credential Manager. Chunks use an audited AEAD implementation (AES-256-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305) with a unique random nonce and authenticated metadata containing schema version, session id, sequence, source kind, timing, codec, byte length, and plaintext digest. Never log plaintext, tokens, calendar content, audio bytes, keys, or signed update secrets.
+
+Write ciphertext and manifest updates to a same-directory temporary file, flush file contents, atomically rename, and flush the parent directory where supported before upload eligibility. Set owner-only permissions/ACLs. Enforce a configurable bounded quota (initial gate: 2 GiB or 8 hours, whichever comes first), surface low disk space before start, and pause capture before data becomes unspoolable. Delete only after validating the authenticated acknowledgement matches session, sequence, and digest.
+
+Desktop login uses the existing Product-Suite browser-based authentication flow and an app-owned deep link with PKCE/state/nonce. The Rust layer exchanges the one-time result and stores the opaque refresh/session credential in the OS credential store; the webview receives only authentication state. A callback mismatch, expired state, or missing secure store fails closed. Signing and updater private keys exist only in protected release CI, never in the repository or desktop runtime.
+
+### Consent, permissions, and association
+
+The first screen explains mic/system capture, Product-Suite upload, local encrypted recovery, retention/deletion, and the always-visible recording indicator. Preflight tests both sources and shows separate level meters. Start is disabled until the OS permissions are granted and the current Product-Suite actor/meeting authority is confirmed. During recording, both the compact window and tray/menu-bar item display a persistent red indicator, duration, pause, and stop; closing the window does not obscure the tray indicator or stop recording without confirmation.
+
+Calendar association is optional and read-only. The app requests backend-provided suggestions and displays title/time/organizer minimally. A user confirms one event or chooses no event. The collector submits only the selected opaque association identifier alongside the existing meeting/session request. It stores no calendar credential and never starts, schedules, or resumes a recording from an event.
+
+## Tauri security and release design
+
+- Define one capability for the main recorder window and only the commands it needs. Do not use wildcards, shell, filesystem, process, global HTTP, clipboard, or arbitrary URL permissions. Updater permission is isolated to an explicit user/mandatory-update flow.
+- Apply a strict CSP with packaged local assets only; no remote scripts, `eval`, or navigation. Allow only the Product-Suite API/update origins in Rust configuration, validated against immutable release configuration.
+- Pin exact Tauri/Rust/plugin and JavaScript dependency versions in `Cargo.lock` and `bun.lock`; run dependency license/advisory checks and inspect capability changes in review.
+- Windows CI builds on Windows with MSVC and produces one documented installer format (prefer signed NSIS for MVP). Sign the executable and installer with the approved organization certificate/provider, timestamp it, and verify signatures on a clean supported VM.
+- macOS CI builds/signs both Apple Silicon and Intel artifacts (or a verified universal artifact) with Developer ID Application, hardened runtime, required audio entitlements only, notarizes, staples, and validates Gatekeeper on clean hosts. Ad-hoc signing is not releasable.
+- Enable the Tauri updater only with HTTPS endpoints and its embedded public key. Tauri update signatures are mandatory and cannot be disabled. CI signs every update artifact with the protected private key; the client rejects missing, invalid, replayed/downgraded, wrong-platform, and wrong-channel metadata. Key backup and rotation/recovery are release prerequisites.
+- A critical mandatory update blocks starting a new recording but must still allow the user to drain/export diagnostics or explicitly discard an existing encrypted spool. An update never interrupts active capture or drain.
+
+## Performance and reliability budgets
+
+These are release gates, measured on a representative low-spec Windows 10/11 x86_64 machine and Intel/Apple Silicon macOS machines:
+
+| Gate | Budget |
+|---|---|
+| Idle private working set | p95 <= 80 MiB after 10 minutes |
+| Recording private working set | p95 <= 120 MiB over a 60-minute session, growth <= 10 MiB after warm-up |
+| Idle CPU | p95 <= 1% of one logical core |
+| Recording CPU | p95 <= 8% of one logical core; no sustained thermal warning |
+| Packaged app excluding system webview | <= 25 MiB compressed per architecture unless measured exception is approved |
+| Chunk duration | 5–10 seconds; upload concurrency <= 2 per session |
+| Audio continuity | no unexplained missing/overlapping monotonic interval; every known gap is explicit evidence |
+| Crash/network recovery | no acknowledged chunk re-transcribed; no unacknowledged durable chunk lost |
+| Stop-to-finalize | p95 <= 30 seconds on healthy broadband after final chunk, excluding transcription provider latency explicitly reported by server |
+| Spool confidentiality | zero plaintext audio/token/key files; logs pass secret/PII scan |
+
+## Observability and support
+
+Emit privacy-safe structured events for state transition, platform/source, permission outcome, chunk duration/size, spool depth/age, retry class, acknowledgement latency, recovery result, drain duration, updater result, and app/build version. Use randomly generated diagnostic session correlation, not audio/calendar text. Keep bounded rotating local logs, provide a user-reviewed diagnostics export, and route hosted telemetry through the existing Product-Suite observability contract only after consent. Metrics must distinguish capture, spool, upload, server acknowledgement, transcription, and finalization so failures are not mislabeled.
+
+## Three medium stacked PRs
+
+1. **PR 1 — Secure Tauri shell and proven native capture.** Owns the new desktop package, narrow Tauri capabilities/CSP, auth state surface, explicit consent/permission preflight, visible indicator, typed audio abstraction, Windows WASAPI loopback, macOS Core Audio Tap, mic input, and platform proof harnesses. It does not upload or persist recordings.
+2. **PR 2 — Encrypted durable delivery.** Stacks on PR 1 and on landed durability plus `LocalCaptureSource` contracts. Owns the encrypted spool state machine, OS key storage, Product-Suite authenticated client, idempotent upload/ack/resume, crash recovery, quota/backoff, and local evidence projection. It does not finalize, interpret evidence, or derive actions.
+3. **PR 3 — Finalization, optional association, and signed distribution.** Stacks on PR 2 and landed terminal-finalization/calendar-recognition contracts. Owns user-confirmed association, stop/drain/finalize UX, artifact handoff/deep link, signed installers, notarization, mandatory signed updater, release provenance/SBOM, and cross-platform E2E/performance gates.
+
+Each PR must be independently reviewable and keep all earlier tests green. No PR may widen another lane’s ownership merely to make its stack pass.
+
+## Rollout and rollback
+
+Start with signed internal builds against a non-production Product-Suite environment, then an allowlisted cohort, then a small percentage stable channel. Server feature flags may disable new session creation by minimum app version/platform while continuing to accept retries and finalize already-created sessions. Record crash-free session rate, permission success, gap rate, retry/spool age, finalization success, and memory/CPU budgets before each expansion.
+
+Rollback is a forward signed updater release plus server-side start kill switch; never distribute unsigned downgrades or delete a user’s recoverable spool. If a capture regression appears, block new starts, let active/recoverable sessions drain on their compatible API, and ship a higher-version signed rollback build. Server contracts remain backward compatible for at least the declared spool recovery window. Credential/signing compromise invokes the documented key rotation/revocation procedure and blocks releases until trust is restored.
+
+## Human prerequisites and stop conditions
+
+Before DEV PR 3, a human must provide/approve the Apple Developer team and Developer ID certificate, notarization credentials, Windows organization signing provider/certificate, protected updater signing key custody and backup, distribution domains, privacy copy, retention limits, and release-channel ownership. Do not substitute developer/ad-hoc certificates.
+
+Return to PLAN if the Core Audio Tap proof fails, an upstream API cannot preserve source topology or idempotent acknowledgements, the app requires pre-14.2 macOS/Linux, a new local database/service is proposed, or any requirement grants the collector candidate/task/tenant authority.
